INTRO=[heading('Introduction'),paragraph('In geometry conic section is also called a conic. Any curve produced by the intersection of plane and right circle cone depending on the angle of plane and right circular cone. The “conic section” comes from the fact that the principle type of conic section known as ellipses, hyperbolas and parabola are generated by cutting a cone with a plane. Most modern textbooks of calculus depart from this geometrical approach, instead conic section are defined as some types of loci and studied through analytic geometry. In this unit we will study an approach to conic section which are defined as the intersection of two cones. Then the vertices of two cones becomes the inherent foci of the conic section and directrix exists associated with each of the inherent foci. At the end of this unit we will learn about the properties through real life examples.'),note('The source’s “intersection of two cones” and identification of cone vertices with conic foci are not the usual conic construction. A plane intersects a double cone; the cone’s common vertex is not generally a focus of the resulting curve. Focus–directrix definitions describe conics analytically and require their own geometric construction. The readable source claims are retained here rather than silently rewritten.')]
PAGES=[page('111_page_1.jpg',213,INTRO+[heading('8.1 Conics and members of its family'),paragraph('A conic section (or simply conic) is a curve obtained as the intersection of the surface of a cone with a plane. The three types of conic sections are the hyperbola, the parabola, and the ellipse. The circle is type of ellipse, and is sometimes considered to be a fourth type of conic section.')]),
 page('111_page_2.jpg',214,[paragraph('Conic sections can be generated by intersecting a plane with a cone. A cone has two identically shaped parts called nappes. One nappe is what most people mean by “cone,” and has the shape of a party hat. Conic sections are generated by the intersection of a plane with a cone. If the plane is parallel to the axis of revolution (the y-axis), then the conic section is a hyperbola. If the plane is parallel to the generating line, the conic section is a parabola. If the plane is perpendicular to the axis of revolution, the conic section is a circle. If the plane intersects one nappe at an angle to the axis (other than 90°), then the conic section is an ellipse.'),conic_family(),paragraph('In this unit, we look at one of these conic sections that are the circles.'),
 note('Figure 8.1 preserves the source’s three schematic double-cone drawings, section labels and relative cutting-plane directions. The nondegenerate classifications assume that the plane avoids the cone vertex. An ellipse requires a cutting angle steeper than a generating line, not simply any angle other than 90°; a circle is its perpendicular special case. Planes through the vertex can give a point, a single generator or a pair of generators.'),
 heading('8.2 Circle'),paragraph('A circle is a shape that has a continuous and constant curve. Though it is always curving; it has an algebraic expression that describes its nature.'),heading('8.2.1 Circle and its equation in standard form'),paragraph('The set of all points in the plane in such a way that its distances from a fixed point in that plane (called the center) is equal to a fixed distance (called the radius) of the circle.'),heading('Derivation of Circle Equation'),paragraph('This definition helps us in developing a standard form of the equation of a circle. Let C(h,k) be a fixed point at the center of the circle and r is the radius of the circle and P(x,y) is any one of the collection of points on the circumference of the circle that gives the distance from the fixed point C(h,k) which is called the radius of a circle. The position vectors of P and C relative to origin are respectively.'),eq(r'\overrightarrow{OP}=(x,y),\quad\overrightarrow{OC}=(h,k)','i'),circle_radius(),paragraph('From the Figure 8.2, the distance from the center C to point P is the fixed distance equals the radius of the circle:'),eq(r'\overrightarrow{OC}+\overrightarrow{CP}=\overrightarrow{OP}'),eq(r'\overrightarrow{CP}=\overrightarrow{OP}-\overrightarrow{OC}'),eq(r'\overrightarrow{CP}=(x,y)-(h,k)=(x-h,y-k)'),eq(r'|CP|=\sqrt{(x-h)^2+(y-k)^2},\quad\mathrm{distance\ formula}'),eq(r'|CP|^2=(\sqrt{(x-h)^2+(y-k)^2})^2,\quad\mathrm{squaring\ both\ sides}'),eq(r'r^2=(x-h)^2+(y-k)^2,\quad |CP|^2=(CP)^2=r^2'),note('The circle definition requires r > 0 for a nondegenerate circle. At r = 0 the equation describes the single point C; a negative alleged radius cannot be a distance. Vector CP is the displacement, while |CP| is its nonnegative length. The final scalar (CP)² notation means squared length, not a componentwise vector square.')])]
